\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb}

\begin{document}

\section*{Basis for the Image Space of Fourth-Degree Fermionic Operators}

\subsection*{Corrected Basis Construction}

The image space of $\text{Span}\{a_j^* a_k^* a_l a_m\}$ in the Clifford algebra has the following basis:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Degree-2 Majorana monomials}:
    \[
    \mathcal{B}_2 = \{c_\mu c_\nu \mid \mu < \nu\}
    \]
    Generated by operators with \textit{one repeated index} (e.g., $a_j^* a_k^* a_j a_l$).
    
    \item \textbf{Degree-4 Majorana monomials}:
    \[
    \mathcal{B}_4 = \{c_\mu c_\nu c_\rho c_\sigma \mid \mu < \nu < \rho < \sigma\}
    \]
    Includes:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure degree-4 terms (from all-distinct-index operators)
        \item The degree-4 part of $a_j^* a_k^* a_j a_k$
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
\textbf{Note}: The coupled $\mathbf{1}$ term from $a_j^* a_k^* a_j a_k$ is absorbed into $\mathcal{B}_4$ coefficients and does not appear as an independent basis element.

\subsection*{Why the Identity is Excluded}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Linear independence}: No combination of $\mathcal{B}_2 \cup \mathcal{B}_4$ can produce $\mathbf{1}$
    \item \textbf{Physical reason}: Normal-ordered $a_j^* a_k^* a_l a_m$ annihilates the vacuum $|0\rangle$
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Example: N=2 Fermionic Modes}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Degree-2 basis (6 elements):
    \[
    \{c_1c_2, c_1c_3, c_1c_4, c_2c_3, c_2c_4, c_3c_4\}
    \]
    \item Degree-4 basis (1 element):
    \[
    \{c_1c_2c_3c_4\}
    \]
    \item Total dimension: $6 + 1 = 7$
\end{itemize}

\noindent
\textbf{Example operator}: 
\[
a_1^* a_2^* a_1 a_2 \mapsto -\tfrac{1}{8}\mathbf{1} + \tfrac{1}{8}c_1c_2c_3c_4
\]
This is expressible through $\mathcal{B}_4$ alone (the $\mathbf{1}$ term appears only as a coefficient).

\subsection*{Conclusion}

The image space basis is:
\[
\mathcal{B} = \mathcal{B}_2 \cup \mathcal{B}_4
\]
with \textit{no standalone identity term}. This structure properly captures all fourth-degree fermionic operators while maintaining linear independence.

